<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">French Polynesia</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – present · capital Papeete</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's Pacific overseas collectivity of 118 islands around Tahiti, shaped by three decades of nuclear testing and a long debate over autonomy and independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1946 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-establishments-in-oceania">French Establishments in Oceania</a> became an overseas territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-fourth-republic">French Union</a>, and in 1957 it was renamed French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. The nationalist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pouvanaa-a-oopa">Pouvanaa a Oopa</a>, who campaigned for independence, was arrested in 1958 and exiled after a disputed conviction.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 1966 to 1996 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> carried out 193 nuclear tests at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moruroa">Moruroa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fangataufa">Fangataufa</a> atolls, first in the atmosphere and later underground, bringing money and jobs but lasting health and environmental harm. The resumption of testing in 1995 caused riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeete</a>. Statutes of 1984, 1996 and 2004 extended autonomy, and politics was long a contest between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-flosse">Gaston Flosse</a>, favouring ties to France, and the pro-independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-temaru">Oscar Temaru</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> is an overseas collectivity with its own president and assembly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> relisted it as a non-self-governing territory in 2013. Tourism and black pearls are the main earners alongside French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-polynesia.jpg" alt="French Polynesia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Polynesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Papeete</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gaston Flosse, Oscar Temaru</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Tahitian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Polynésie française</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1986&amp;focus=french-polynesia" aria-label="French Polynesia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="618 2829 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Polynesia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M938 3085L938 3085M938 3085L5054 728M938 3085L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M938 3085h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M938 3085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M938 3085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Papeete. <a href="/atlas?year=1986&amp;focus=french-polynesia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
